{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-fcda0275-b090-4819-9752-9603b1f368e0","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-916abaea6991873a","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-9010-cvflgesc/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9010-E002.md","content":"# Emit audit events for KServe inference requests with reliable at-least-once delivery\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic implements inference audit logging in the KServe serving stack: every inference request through a KServe endpoint emits a structured audit event conforming to the published common audit event schema, and events are delivered to the cluster audit sink with at-least-once semantics that survive component restarts.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Emit one audit event per inference request through a KServe endpoint, capturing caller identity, model/version, timestamp, and response status per the common audit event schema. No request or response payloads are recorded.\n- Deliver audit events to the cluster audit sink (the standard cluster logging forwarder) with at-least-once semantics.\n- Buffer or otherwise persist in-flight audit events so they are not lost when the emitting component restarts.\n- Honor the per-project audit enablement contract: inference auditing emits events only for projects where it is enabled.\n- Apply to both serverless and raw deployment modes of InferenceService, consistent with the platform's serving architecture.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- For a sample of inference requests against a KServe endpoint, each request produces an audit event containing caller identity, model/version, timestamp, and response status, conforming to the published schema, with no payload data present in the event.\n- With auditing enabled for a project, killing and restarting the emitting component mid-traffic results in no audit event loss (at-least-once delivery verified by comparing emitted requests against events received at the cluster audit sink).\n- With auditing disabled for a project via the enablement contract, inference requests in that project produce no audit events; with it enabled, they do.\n- Events arrive at the standard cluster logging forwarder and are structured (machine-parseable) per the schema.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-1 (P0): Every inference request through a KServe endpoint emits an audit event capturing caller identity, model/version, timestamp, and response status (not payloads).\n- HLR-2 (P0): Audit events are delivered to the cluster audit sink with at-least-once semantics and are not lost on component restart.\n\n## Risks / Notes\n\n- Delivery validation assumes the cluster audit sink (standard cluster logging forwarder) is available in the target cluster; if absent, HLR-2 validation cannot complete. This is an environment prerequisite, not a deliverable of this epic.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":27191,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a383d082c6eb5018","agent_id":"a227ee329201cc433","session_id":"ec977243-d2eb-4166-aef8-f740d35bd2a8","uuid":"c91f0503-e275-47f7-844b-704450d55f3e","timestamp":"2026-10-22T14:03:07.437Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-9010 into epics"}
