{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a0c1e3338a152db8","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9010-E001\n3\ttitle: Define and publish the common inference audit event schema and per-project\n4\t  enablement contract\n5\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9010\n6\tcomponent: KServe\n7\tteam: RHOAI Serving\n8\ttype: Implementation\n9\tpriority: P0\n10\tdependencies: []\n11\tai_signals:\n12\t  change_specificity: 1\n13\t  pattern_precedent: 0\n14\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n15\t  existing_foundation: 0\n16\t  open_questions: 1\n17\t  external_dependency: 1\n18\t  human_process_gates: 0\n19\t  repo_access: 1\n20\t  architecture_claims: 1\n21\timplementation_type: null\n22\tinvestigation_signals: null\n23\tai_implementability: High\n24\tai_implementability_score: 5\n25\tjira_key: null\n26\tbranch: null\n27\tgated_by: null\n28\tgate_failure_impact: null\n29\t---\n30\t# Define and publish the common inference audit event schema and per-project enablement contract\n31\t\n32\t## Description\n33\t\n34\tThis epic produces the shared contract that all inference auditing work builds against: a common audit event schema covering the fields auditors require, plus the definition of how per-project audit enablement is represented so the serving path and the Dashboard build against one contract rather than inventing separate surfaces. The schema is published so that future components (pipelines, workbenches) can emit compatible events.\n35\t\n36\t## Scope\n37\t\n38\t- Define the audit event schema capturing, for every inference request: caller identity, model and version, timestamp, and response status. Payload content is explicitly excluded from the schema.\n39\t- Define the per-project audit enablement contract: how the enabled/disabled state for inference auditing is represented per project, so the inference audit emission path and the Dashboard administration control both build against the same interface. The specific mechanism remains an implementation decision for the serving team.\n40\t- Publish the schema in the location the serving team designates so future components can consume and conform to it.\n41\t- Ensure the schema is structured (machine-parseable) and tamper-evident event content is representable, consistent with the compliance driver in the strategy.\n42\t\n43\t## Acceptance Criteria\n44\t\n45\t- A schema document exists that defines a representation for caller identity, model/version, timestamp, and response status, and explicitly excludes request/response payloads.\n46\t- The per-project enablement contract is documented precisely enough that the emission implementation and the Dashboard control can be built against it without further design negotiation.\n47\t- The schema is published and versioned in an accessible location for downstream components.\n48\t- The schema has been reviewed and agreed by the serving team, with the review outcome recorded.\n49\t\n50\t## HLR Traceability\n51\t\n52\t- HLR-4 (P2): A common audit event schema is defined and published so future components emit compatible events. This epic also unblocks HLR-1, which requires events to conform to this schema.\n53\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8651f2c66d4800a1","agent_id":"a2675c8e1f3ec1496","session_id":"ec977243-d2eb-4166-aef8-f740d35bd2a8","uuid":"9b3c1e35-9a1a-45fe-86dd-32e8901803a5","timestamp":"2026-10-22T14:07:18.821Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a0c1e3338a152db8","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9010 decomposition"}
