{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ae30b9aa480a4a5d","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-9010\n3\tscore: 14\n4\tpass: true\n5\trecommendation: accept\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: minor\n8\t  criterion: Completeness\n9\t  description: The problem statement requires tamper-evident audit events and E001\n10\t    scope mentions tamper-evident content representability, but no epic acceptance\n11\t    criterion verifies the tamper-evidence property. Add an AC to E001 (e.g., schema\n12\t    review confirms tamper-evident representability) or to E002 covering delivery-side\n13\t    integrity.\n14\terror: null\n15\t---\n16\t## Review Summary\n17\t\n18\tScore: 14/14 — pass\n19\tRecommendation: accept\n20\t\n21\tContract validator: OK (body sections, canonical component names, Rule 11 docs-last\n22\tcoverage, and priority inheritance all pass). Triage consistency: `triage` is null\n23\t(full decomposition); 4 epics is expected output, no contradiction.\n24\t\n25\tThe decomposition is a clean translation of the strategy: all five HLRs mapped with\n26\ta traceability matrix, a correctly parallelized E002/E003 pair behind the shared\n27\tE001 contract, Rule 11 docs-last edges complete, and the strategy's priority\n28\tinversion on HLR-4 explicitly resolved via priority inheritance and flagged as a\n29\thealth warning. One minor completeness nit (tamper-evidence not verified by any AC)\n30\tdoes not affect the score.\n31\t\n32\t## Criterion Details\n33\t\n34\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n35\tAll five HLRs map to at least one epic: HLR-1 and HLR-2 (P0) → E002, HLR-3 (P1) →\n36\tE003, HLR-4 (P2) → E001, HLR-5 (P2) → E004. The traceability matrix is present in\n37\tthe summary and every epic has an HLR Traceability section. Priority inheritance is\n38\tcorrect: E001 blocks all P0 work (E002) and is P0; E003 is P1 and gates no P0 work;\n39\tE004 is a `docs-authoring` epic whose P0 derives from the strategy frontmatter\n40\t`priority: Critical`, correctly exempt from inheritance. No priority collapse:\n41\tHLR-1 and HLR-2 are one logical P0 change on one emission surface, and HLR-4's\n42\tschema is explicitly non-deferrable (HLR-1 cannot ship before it is settled), so\n43\tbundling it into the P0-blocking E001 is correct, not collapse.\n44\t\n45\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n46\tFrontmatter dependencies match the summary DAG diagram edge-for-edge (E001→E002,\n47\tE001→E003, E001→E004, E002→E004, E003→E004), with arrows drawn dependency→dependent\n48\tper convention. Each edge is justified: E001→E002 and E001→E003 are Rule 13\n49\tbuild-against edges (the schema and enablement contract are artifacts the consuming\n50\tcode builds against — the strategy itself states HLR-1 cannot ship before the\n51\tschema is settled); the three edges into E004 are Rule 11 docs-authoring-last.\n52\tE002 and E003 are correctly parallel (different repos; the shared contract is fully\n53\tdefined in E001), with no unjustified serializing edge between them. No cycles, no\n54\tedges to nonexistent epics, no konflux/license pairings to check, no branch files.\n55\tCross-checking epic content found no uncaptured data/artifact dependency: every\n56\tconsumer of the schema or enablement contract has an edge to E001. Critical path\n57\tlength 3 (E001→E002→E004) is within the M-strategy heuristic (3-4).\n58\t\n59\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n60\tEach epic has a single component/team tuple: E001/E002 (KServe, RHOAI Serving),\n61\tE003 (Dashboard, RHOAI Dashboard), E004 (Documentation, RHOAI Documentation). Work-\n62\tproduct types are correctly separated — code implementation and documentation\n63\tauthoring are in different epics. No scope duplication: the schema and enablement\n64\tcontract are defined once in E001 and consumed by E002/E003; no two epics\n65\tindependently build the same data structure, UI surface, or API. No epic exceeds\n66\t~2 weeks of work.\n67\t\n68\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n69\tAll four epics are Implementation and each produces a concrete artifact (published\n70\tschema/contract, emission pipeline, Dashboard control, published documentation).\n71\tHaving no Investigation epics is justified: the strategy's Open Questions section\n72\texplicitly declares none, and the one genuine uncertainty (cluster logging forwarder\n73\tavailability) is correctly handled as an acceptance criterion plus a risk note on\n74\tE002 rather than an Investigation, since it does not change what gets built. All\n75\t`gated_by`/`gate_failure_impact` fields are null and no epic lists an Investigation\n76\tin dependencies, so no gating metadata issues exist.\n77\t\n78\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n79\tAll epics are Implementation type and correctly carry `ai_signals` (not\n80\t`investigation_signals`); ai-signals files exist for all four epics. Every\n81\tfrontmatter signal value matches its rationale file, and the rationales are\n82\tconsistent with the strategy content: E001's greenfield contract scores\n83\t(existing_foundation 0, adapter_pattern 0) fit schema-definition work; E002/E003\n84\tscore pattern_precedent +1 and adapter_pattern +1 against the existing KServe\n85\trequest logger and Dashboard settings surfaces; E004's human_process_gates -1\n86\tcorrectly reflects the docs editorial/technical review gates. E002's\n87\texternal_dependency 0 with the sink-availability caveat moved to a risk note is a\n88\tdefensible coordination-vs-environment distinction. No signal contradicts the\n89\trubric conditions.\n90\t\n91\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n92\tEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the strategy, including a concrete\n93\tkill-and-restart at-least-once verification for HLR-2 and an enable/disable\n94\tbehavioral check for the per-project contract. Rule-mandated ACs: E004\n95\t(docs-authoring) has the Rule 24 \"technically reviewed against implementation\" AC.\n96\tRule 23 (rollback/feature-flag) does not apply — this is a 