{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-92b9f0b68326db15","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9010\n3\tepic_count: 4\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9010 (Audit logging for model inference traffic)\n11\t\n12\t## Epic List\n13\t\n14\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n15\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n16\t| RHAISTRAT-9010-E001 | Define and publish the common inference audit event schema and per-project enablement contract | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n17\t| RHAISTRAT-9010-E002 | Emit audit events for KServe inference requests with reliable at-least-once delivery | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n18\t| RHAISTRAT-9010-E003 | Dashboard administration control for per-project inference auditing | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n19\t| RHAISTRAT-9010-E004 | Document enabling inference auditing and interpreting audit events | Implementation | RHOAI Documentation | P0 |\n20\t\n21\t## Dependency DAG\n22\t\n23\t```mermaid\n24\tgraph TD\n25\t    E001[RHAISTRAT-9010-E001 Schema and enablement contract] --> E002[RHAISTRAT-9010-E002 KServe audit emission]\n26\t    E001 --> E003[RHAISTRAT-9010-E003 Dashboard per-project toggle]\n27\t    E002 --> E004[RHAISTRAT-9010-E004 Documentation]\n28\t    E003 --> E004\n29\t    E001 --> E004\n30\t```\n31\t\n32\t## DAG Justification\n33\t\n34\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n35\t|------|------|-----------|\n36\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 13 (build-against artifact) | The emission implementation builds against the published audit event schema; the strategy states HLR-1 cannot ship before the HLR-4 schema is settled. |\n37\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 13 (build-against artifact) | The Dashboard toggle writes the per-project enablement state that the serving path consumes; both sides build against the contract defined alongside the schema so neither epic invents the shared surface independently. |\n38\t| E002 ∥ E003 | Rule 14 (different repos, no shared artifacts) | KServe midstream repo and the Dashboard repo are independent; the shared contract is fully defined in E001, so the two implementations run in parallel. |\n39\t| E001 → E004, E002 → E004, E003 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring always last) | Documentation describes what was built and must reflect the schema, the emission behavior, and the Dashboard control. |\n40\t\n41\tNo Investigation epics: the strategy's Open Questions section is explicit that none remain, and the audit sink interface is the standard cluster logging forwarder. No conditional ADRs or pending reviews are present. No onboarding chain: both KServe and Dashboard exist in architecture context and are Konflux-onboarded.\n42\t\n43\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n44\t\n45\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n46\t|-----|----------|---------|\n47\t| HLR-1 (audit event per inference request) | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9010-E002 |\n48\t| HLR-2 (at-least-once delivery, no loss on restart) | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9010-E002 |\n49\t| HLR-3 (per-project enable/disable from Dashboard) | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9010-E003 |\n50\t| HLR-4 (common audit event schema published) | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9010-E001 |\n51\t| HLR-5 (documentation) | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9010-E004 |\n52\t\n53\tAll five HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements. HLR-1 and HLR-2 are one logical change in one component (the audit emission pipeline and its delivery guarantees) and share the same implementation surface, so they are covered by a single epic rather than split.\n54\t\n55\t## Priority Notes\n56\t\n57\t- E001 is labeled P0 by priority inheritance: it gates all P0 work (HLR-1/HLR-2 via E002), and an epic blocking all P0 work is implicitly P0. The strategy itself acknowledges this dependency in HLR-4's note.\n58\t- E004 is a `docs-authoring` epic. Per the docs-authoring priority exception it does not inherit from downstream HLRs; its priority is derived from the strategy frontmatter `priority: Critical` → P0.\n59\t- E003 is P1, matching HLR-3; it gates no P0 work.\n60\t\n61\t## Health Warnings\n62\t\n63\t- **Priority inversion (strategy-level)**: HLR-4 is labeled P2 in the strategy while its own note states HLR-1 (P0) cannot ship before the schema is settled. The decomposition resolves this by promoting the schema epic to P0 via priority inheritance; the strategy's P2 label understates the schema's criticality.\n64\t- **Missing strategy sections**: the strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then Acceptance Criteria section, and no Non-Functional Requirements section. Decomposition proceeded with available information; owning teams were derived from architecture context (KServe → RHOAI Serving, Dashboard → RHOAI Dashboard). Per-epic acceptance criteria were derived from the HLRs and problem statement.\n65\t- **Assumption (not a scope trap)**: the strategy asserts the cluster audit sink is the standard cluster logging forwarder and treats its availability as given. If a target cluster lacks the logging forwarder, HLR-2 delivery validation is impacted; this is captured as an acceptance criterion and risk note on the emission epic rather than an Investigation, since the strategy explicitly declares no open questions.\n66\t\n67\t## Tiered Delivery\n68\t\n69\tNot applicable: there are no external dependencies with uncertain timing. All work is internal to the platform components.\n70\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b6f412bc8c7d7b8d","agent_id":"a7fa4a86cfe90d783","session_id":"ec977243-d2eb-4166-aef8-f740d35bd2a8","uuid":"0ebada59-b8d4-4460-80bc-ec106d8575f0","timestamp":"2026-10-22T14:11:46.291Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-92b9f0b68326db15","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-9010 revision"}
